     "derivation": "The Avestan word for 'beaver' is bawra- m. (Bartholomae AiW col. 925), with the by-form bawray-/bawri- m. 'beaver', f. 'she-beaver' and the adjective bawraini-/bawrinay- 'made of beaver'; all three occur in the one passage Yt. 5.129 about Anāhitā's coat of 'three hundred she-beavers'. The stem continues *bʰebʰru-/*bʰeru- 'the brown one', beside Skt. babhrú- 'reddish-brown', Lat. fiber, OHG bibar. Nourai's second and third forms, bawraini and bawri, are Bartholomae's exactly. His FIRST form, however, is printed 'bowra' — with -o- — which is not a possible Avestan shape and is contradicted within his own box by 'bawraini, bawri'. The printed page (PDF p.90) does read 'bowra', so this is the book's own misprint for bawra-, not a scanning error; the extraction is faithful. The gloss 'red, brown animal, beaver' is right (Bartholomae 'Biber', Pokorny 'av. bawra-, bawri- \"Biber\"').",
