     "derivation": "The Arabic word travelled west with the Arab pharmacopoeia: Gerard of Cremona (12th c., Toledo) transcribes it baurach, whence Medieval Latin baurach/borax and Old French/French borax, with the Iberian and Italian doublets Spanish borraj/borrax and Italian borrace. Pihan's Dictionnaire étymologique des mots de la langue française dérivés de l'arabe, du persan ou du turc, p.71, gives the entry as 'BORAX, s. m. (a.-p.) بورق [baurak] nitre, en persan بوره [boûrah]' — the label '(a.-p.)' meaning 'Arabic, from Persian', exactly the route Nourai draws. Devic adds that the word entered the European languages from the Arabs about the 9th century.",
