     "id": 5, "lang": "English", "words": "borax, boric, Boron", "verdict": "confirmed",
     "derivation": "borax is attested in English from the late 14th century, taken from Anglo-French boras (Anglo-Norman boreis) < Medieval Latin baurach < Arabic būraq; Middle English boras was later re-latinised to borax. The adjective boric (and the older boracic) is formed on the same stem; Boron is Humphry Davy's coinage of 1812 — he first called the element boracium because he obtained it from boracic acid, then cut it to boron on the model of carbon. So the whole English box is a straight continuation of the French/Latin form, and the capital B on 'Boron' is simply Nourai marking a chemical element.",
