     "derivation": "New Persian ببر with the two fatḥas, babar, is the (now archaic) word for the beaver, the regular reflex of MP babrag with loss of the suffix -ag; it is kept apart from ببر babr 'tiger', which continues MP babr [bpl] and is itself suspected of being a Semitic/Akkadian culture-word. Borhān-e Qāṭe' p.231 describes the babar exactly as a beaver-like fur animal — 'جانوری باشد صحرایی شبیه بگربه لیکن دم ندارد و از پوست آن پوستین سازند' — and adds 'و بسکون ثانی نام درنده‌ای است مشهور', i.e. with sukūn (babr) it is the well-known predator. Mo'in's footnote at p.314 makes the etymological connection Nourai draws: Av. bawra 'basically means red … and was then applied to the animal which in Persian is called ببر (with two fatḥas)'. Nourai's second gloss, 'red fox', is Mo'in's 'روباه' from that same footnote.",
